  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>

  <h2>How do I report suspected elder abuse?</h2>
  <p>Call your state&rsquo;s Adult Protective Services (APS) intake line. If you don&rsquo;t know the number, call the Eldercare Locator at <strong>1-800-677-1116</strong> &mdash; it will route you to the right state agency. For a nursing home or assisted living concern, you can also contact your state&rsquo;s Long-Term Care Ombudsman, accessible through the same number. In an emergency, call 911. Many states allow anonymous reports, and most states grant immunity from civil liability to anyone making a good-faith report.</p>

  <h2>What counts as "actionable" neglect?</h2>
  <p>Civilly actionable neglect generally requires (1) a duty of care (typically created by the facility&rsquo;s admission contract and OBRA / state nursing home regulations), (2) a breach of that duty (failure to deliver care that a reasonably prudent provider would have), (3) causation (the breach contributed to a specific harm), and (4) damages. Documentation matters: facility records, CMS surveys, F-Tag deficiencies, APS findings, ombudsman complaints, and medical records together build the case. Isolated minor lapses without resulting injury usually aren&rsquo;t actionable; patterns of inadequate care with documented harm are.</p>

  <h2>What is an F-Tag and why do attorneys focus on it?</h2>
  <p>F-Tags are the coded deficiency categories used by CMS surveyors when inspecting Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes. F-Tag F686 covers pressure ulcers; F689 covers accident hazards and supervision (falls, wandering); F692 covers nutrition and hydration; F600&ndash;F610 cover abuse, neglect, exploitation, and reporting failures. A facility&rsquo;s F-Tag survey history is a goldmine of evidence: prior deficiencies in the same area establish notice and pattern, which substantially strengthens both liability and any heightened-conduct showing needed for statutory enhancements. CMS survey results are publicly available through the Medicare Care Compare website.</p>

  <h2>What is the statute of limitations on an elder abuse claim?</h2>
  <p>Varies by state and by claim type. Most states apply a 1- to 3-year limitations period for general elder abuse and nursing home negligence claims, sometimes measured from the date of injury and sometimes from the date the injury was discovered (the "discovery rule"). Wrongful death claims often have a separate (usually 1- to 2-year) limitations period running from the date of death. Financial exploitation claims often benefit from the discovery rule because the exploitation may not surface until years after it happened. State-specific elder abuse statutes sometimes provide a longer window. Do not assume; check with an attorney early.</p>

  <h2>If the victim has dementia, can they still sue?</h2>
  <p>Yes, but they need a representative. The court appoints a guardian ad litem or accepts a properly-authorized representative under the state&rsquo;s conservatorship/guardianship law. An existing power of attorney may or may not authorize litigation; some POAs explicitly authorize legal claims, others don&rsquo;t. Where the elder has died, the personal representative of the estate brings the claim. Cognitive impairment is not a barrier to recovery &mdash; in fact, it often strengthens the case because the victim&rsquo;s vulnerability is part of the wrongdoing.</p>

  <h2>Can a family member sue if the elder has died?</h2>
  <p>Yes, through two parallel legal vehicles. The state&rsquo;s wrongful death statute lets statutorily-defined family members (typically spouse and children) recover for loss of companionship and economic support, and funeral costs. The state&rsquo;s survival statute lets the decedent&rsquo;s estate recover the damages the decedent could have recovered if alive (pre-death medical bills, etc.). In California uniquely, &sect; 15657 lets heirs continue to recover the decedent&rsquo;s pre-death pain and suffering &mdash; an important departure from the usual rule that pain-and-suffering damages don&rsquo;t survive the victim&rsquo;s death.</p>

  <h2>How long does discovery of financial exploitation usually take?</h2>
  <p>Often years. Many exploitation schemes &mdash; particularly those involving a trusted family member with power of attorney &mdash; aren&rsquo;t discovered until the elder&rsquo;s death triggers a review of finances by other family members. The discovery rule, where applicable, can extend the limitations period to the date of reasonable discovery. Strong cases often involve forensic accountant work reconstructing transactions across multiple years and accounts.</p>

  <h2>Do elder abuse attorneys work on contingency?</h2>
  <p>Most do. Contingency fees in nursing home and elder abuse practice typically run 33&ndash;40% of recovery. In states with attorney-fee statutes (California &sect; 15657 is the most prominent), recovered fees may offset the contingency percentage or supplement the recovery, depending on the fee agreement. No-recovery-no-fee is the norm, but reasonable case costs (records review, expert witnesses) are typically advanced by the firm and reimbursed from recovery.</p>

  <h2>The nursing home admission contract has a pre-dispute arbitration clause. Are we stuck?</h2>
  <p>Maybe not. Arbitration clauses in nursing home contracts are widespread, and their enforceability is one of the first things an experienced elder abuse attorney analyzes. Defenses to enforcement include (1) the signer lacked authority (a family member signed but didn&rsquo;t have power of attorney for the resident); (2) the resident lacked capacity at signing; (3) the clause is procedurally or substantively unconscionable under the controlling state law; (4) state-specific rules (some states bar mandatory pre-dispute arbitration for nursing home residents on Medicaid); and (5) federal regulatory developments (CMS has periodically attempted to restrict pre-dispute arbitration in Medicare/Medicaid-certified facilities, with the status varying over time). Treat the clause as analyzable, not as a settled barrier.</p>

  <h2>Are settlements in elder abuse cases taxable?</h2>
  <p>Compensatory damages for physical injury or physical sickness are excluded from taxable income under IRC &sect; 104(a)(2). Damages for emotional distress not linked to physical injury, punitive damages, and lost wages may be taxable. Restitution of stolen funds in financial exploitation cases is generally not income (it&rsquo;s the return of the elder&rsquo;s own money). Consult a tax professional for specific situations.</p>

  <h2>Can the facility retaliate against the resident for complaining?</h2>
  <p>Retaliation is prohibited by federal law for any resident in a Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing home (OBRA / 42 CFR Part 483 protects against transfer, discharge, or retaliation for exercising any resident right, including complaining and contacting APS or the ombudsman). State laws provide similar protections. If retaliation occurs, that itself becomes an additional claim and is often a fact a jury responds to strongly.</p>

